  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nConfidence remains high in quiet and dry conditions through\nearly Sunday afternoon as high pressure remains in control. With\n850mb temperatures trending within the 80th to 90th percentile\nacross the Great Lakes and East Coast, temperatures will average\nwell above normal, with high reaching into the mid ot upper 60s.\nDespite the unseasonably warm conditions, the potential for\nrecord high temperatures remains low. \n\nLight rain may spread into the region late Sunday as a coastal\nlow, currently near Florida, tracks northward over the next 48\nhours. Any precipitation associated with this system will most\nlikely remain confided east of Pittsburgh, near the Laurel\nHighlands and ridges of West Virginia. Probabilities for at\nleast a tenth of inch of rain ranges from 40-60% east of\nPittsburgh while areas in eastern Ohio may receive no precip. \n\nA cold front is then expected to move through the Midwest and\ninto the Ohio River Valley early Monday morning, bringing the\nreturn to near-seaonsable temperatures and light rain. Mid-level\ndry air advancing from the northwest Monday afternoon will\ndecrease precip chances. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nA lull in precipitation is likely from Tuesday into early\nWednesday as high pressure builds across the Great Lakes,\nsupporting generally quiet weather. \n\nAttention then turns to a potential disturbance developing over\nthe Southeast late Wednesday into Thanksgiving before tracking \nnorthward. Considerable uncertainty remains regarding the \nsystem's track and the potential for winter weather impacts \nacross the region. Ensemble guidance remains varied, with \nsolutions ranging from an all-rain event to transitioning to wet\nsnow, while some members introduce brief periods of wintry mix \nbefore warmer air overspreads the region and chances precip back\nto rain. There is even solutions that have no precipitation \nduring this time period at all. As of now, the probability of \nwinter weather headlines remains low, but not zero (10-20%), \nwith the highest probabilities focused the higher terrain.",
